{0 Product Proof Pipeline Audit}

This audit records what the current implementation does before any redesign of
the product proof obligations. It is intentionally conservative: it identifies
where the current pipeline is soundly structured, where it is only an
approximation, and where a future change must be justified against
{!page-temporal_phase_semantics}.

{1 Product-step enumeration}

[lib/domain/verification/from_model.ml] builds product summaries from the
program transitions and the assumption/guarantee automata.

The important current property is that the builder enumerates the product
combinations and does not prune branches by proving same-step formula
inconsistency. It classifies destinations as safe, bad assumption, or bad
guarantee, then groups cases by:

- program transition index;
- source product state;
- assumption edge.

For each such group, the safe guarantee outcomes are stored in [safe_cases],
and the bad-guarantee outcomes are stored in [unsafe_cases].

{1 Postcondition disjunction}

[lib/domain/verification/post.ml] already keeps the relevant disjunction for a
fixed source precondition. For a product-step summary, it adds an ensured
formula equal to the disjunction of all [safe_cases] guarantee guards.

This means the current missing piece is not "add a disjunction". The
disjunction exists. The open issue is how to justify that the source product
state used by the obligation is genuinely reachable at the corresponding
end-of-instant boundary.

{1 Current pre-generation}

[lib/domain/verification/pre.ml] computes [propagation_requires] with
[guarantee_pre_of_product_state]. The current computation looks at incoming
safe cases and propagates only the safe case's [admissible_guard], shifting
input references forward.

This is a weak compatibility summary for guarantee automaton states. It is not
a full product-step reachability proof. In particular, it deliberately must
not be strengthened by shifting the program guard or the assumption guard into
the next instant: those guards are step-context facts, not end-of-instant
state facts.

This file is therefore the main place where the current approximation can
create spurious source product states in later proof obligations.

{1 Active contract construction}

The implementation does not materialize a second family of Rocq-shaped kernel
clauses. [Ir.product_step_summary] remains the active carrier through [Pre],
[Product_reachability], [Post] and [Temporal_lower].

[Step_contract_projection] then constructs the contracts actually used for
proof:

- safe contracts retain the admissible successor conditions;
- bad-guarantee contracts retain every excluded unsafe condition;
- historical reads have already crossed the typed [Temporal_lower] boundary;
- [Proof_plan] attaches partition provenance and performs prover-independent
  grouping and factorization.

The delicate information remains [propagation_requires], because it comes from
the weak pre-generation described above. Removing the unused kernel-clause
copy does not strengthen or weaken it.

{1 Product boundary}

Explicit product states, product steps and their safe/bad classification are
owned by [Product_build], [Product_types] and [From_model]. No separate export
filters or reorders them before contract construction. In particular, the
active path does not remove an edge by syntactic satisfiability of mixed
source/destination facts.

{1 Things that must not be done}

The following changes would be unsound without a separate formal argument:

- filtering product edges because a conjunction contains both [P] and [not P]
  when [P] may be evaluated before the body and [not P] after the body;
- replacing a step-context program guard by a [pre] fact in a later instant;
- replacing a step-context assumption guard by a [pre] fact in a later
  instant;
- treating [pre^2] as an internal phase marker;
- validating reachability pruning with SMT satisfiability rather than with a
  certified product-reachability argument.

{1 Rejected direction}

Adding a finite product-state coherence predicate [C_p] did not provide the
missing argument in the current model. It only restated information that is
already syntactically known on a product edge, namely its source product state,
and did not give a certified reachability/progression fact for the product
automaton.

The useful lesson is negative: the source state of an edge is not the missing
information. The missing information, if we want to rule out spurious source
states, must be an end-of-instant reachability invariant that is related to
the explored product steps and their progression obligations.

{1 Product reachability decision}

The semantic object is fixed in {!page-temporal_phase_semantics}: graph
reachability in [Product_build] is not enough. The missing object is
[LiveReach(n, p)], semantic reachability of a live product state at the end of
instant [n].

For the current Why3 backend, [LiveReach] must not be represented as a bare
uninterpreted hypothesis. The chosen backend shape is a finite certified
reachability invariant [R_p] for each live product state [p]:

- [R_p] is a first-order end-of-instant formula;
- generated obligations out of [p] may assume [R_p] at [PreviousTick];
- initialization and safe product steps must prove preservation of the
  corresponding [R_p] formulas;
- bad-guarantee steps must be refuted under [R_src] and the current
  step-context facts.

This is deliberately stronger than the rejected [C_p] direction. [C_p] was a
local compatibility predicate over incoming edges. [R_p] is useful only if it
is justified by an induction over product executions, and only if it carries
enough end-of-instant information to rule out spurious source states.

The first implementation step is therefore not to filter product edges. It is
to introduce the invariant shape and its generated preservation/refutation
obligations, then choose a conservative initial synthesis for the [R_p]
formulas.

{1 Prototype status}

The current prototype implements a deliberately small [true]/[false]
synthesis for [R_p]. It is used in two places:

- when [R_dst] is [false], the generator adds preservation obligations of the
  form [guard_dst => false];
- when [R_src] is [false], the Why3 backend adds a backend-local helper
  precondition [false] for obligations whose source is [src].

The backend-local precondition is not part of the global [step] precondition.
It is therefore only a way to avoid proving outgoing obligations from product
states whose unreachability must already be certified by incoming
preservation obligations.

On the original [tests/ok/reactive_alarm_cover.kairos], this removed the
earlier spurious timeouts from unreachable [(Idle, A0, G1)] and
[(Run, A0, G1)] sources and exposed a real model error: from
[(Alarm, A0, G1)], transition [Alarm -> Idle] with [stop] set
[alarmLatched] to [false], while the guarantee state still required the
one-step response [alarmLatched && not motorOn].

The example now separates [AlarmPending] from [Alarm]. [AlarmPending] records
that the controller still owes the one-step response to a hazard seen at the
previous instant. Clearing can only happen once the controller has spent an
instant producing [alarmLatched && not motorOn]. With this model correction,
the [reactive_alarm_cover] obligations validate with the prototype [R_p]
support enabled.
